1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,700
Hey everyone!

2
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:04,705
Today, I want to walk you
through Matrix by HiCode

3
00:00:04,740 --> 00:00:09,580
from connecting your AI resources
to getting actual work done.

4
00:00:10,280 --> 00:00:15,120
The idea behind it is simple:
separate the Brain from the Hand.

5
00:00:15,700 --> 00:00:20,940
The AI handles the reasoning, and an
authorized computer handles the execution.

6
00:00:21,380 --> 00:00:22,700
Let's take a look at how that works.

7
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:27,860
Here on the Matrix website, you'll
see that idea front and center:

8
00:00:28,200 --> 00:00:30,160
Separate the Brain from the Hand.

9
00:00:30,420 --> 00:00:32,040
Keep control of both.

10
00:00:32,980 --> 00:00:36,080
The Brain stays where its AI login lives.

11
00:00:36,640 --> 00:00:40,260
The Hand is the authorized
machine carrying out the work.

12
00:00:40,820 --> 00:00:45,185
Matrix connects your people,
AI resources, and devices,

13
00:00:45,220 --> 00:00:51,020
so you can coordinate tasks and keep
track of what happens along the way.

14
00:00:51,320 --> 00:00:56,000
To get started, you can find the Matrix
desktop client on the download page.

15
00:00:56,300 --> 00:00:58,580
Choose the version for your operating system.

16
00:00:59,240 --> 00:01:01,960
Let's move over to the client and sign in.

17
00:01:02,360 --> 00:01:06,500
The sign-in continues in the browser,
then brings us back to Matrix.

18
00:01:07,420 --> 00:01:08,480
Now we're signed in.

19
00:01:09,120 --> 00:01:13,160
Under Device and Gateway, we can see
this computer and its connection status.

20
00:01:14,020 --> 00:01:16,540
This is the Hand we'll use
to carry out our tasks.

21
00:01:17,120 --> 00:01:19,140
Next, let's look at the AI resources.

22
00:01:19,660 --> 00:01:23,325
Matrix lets you work with resources
available on your own computer,

23
00:01:23,360 --> 00:01:26,560
or choose online resources from the Matrix Pool.

24
00:01:26,860 --> 00:01:28,580
Depending on what your account can access,

25
00:01:28,720 --> 00:01:33,300
the Pool can include company-shared
resources or resources available for rental.

26
00:01:34,020 --> 00:01:38,920
That gives you options for choosing the Brain,
while your authorized device handles the work.

27
00:01:39,380 --> 00:01:42,100
Now, let's open Tasks and add a workspace.

28
00:01:42,640 --> 00:01:45,305
This connects the task to
the project files it needs

29
00:01:45,340 --> 00:01:47,680
and gives us a place to check the results.

30
00:01:48,420 --> 00:01:53,145
For the first task, we'll ask Matrix
to create a folder called "matrix-demo"

31
00:01:53,180 --> 00:01:57,825
and add a file named "project-notes.txt."

32
00:01:57,860 --> 00:02:03,220
That file should contain five realistic updates
about an AI software development project.

33
00:02:03,860 --> 00:02:07,060
We enter the request, create
the task, and start it.

34
00:02:07,420 --> 00:02:11,280
While that's running, let's talk about
the Brain and the Hand for a second.

35
00:02:11,720 --> 00:02:15,260
When we use AI, we often focus
on which model to choose.

36
00:02:15,780 --> 00:02:19,785
But there's another part of the job:
which computer actually does the work,

37
00:02:19,820 --> 00:02:22,160
and which project files it works with.

38
00:02:22,660 --> 00:02:25,140
Matrix lets you think about those separately.

39
00:02:26,140 --> 00:02:31,940
You choose the AI resource for the reasoning,
and an authorized device handles the execution.

40
00:02:32,480 --> 00:02:36,820
Here, we can follow the task as it
runs, then check the files it produces.

41
00:02:37,400 --> 00:02:39,820
That's the connection we're
looking at in this demo.

42
00:02:40,220 --> 00:02:41,040
Let's check the output.

43
00:02:41,480 --> 00:02:44,760
Here's the folder, and inside
it is our project-notes file.

44
00:02:45,140 --> 00:02:47,640
Open it up, and we can see the five updates.

45
00:02:47,840 --> 00:02:51,925
We now have an actual file in
the workspace—and we're going

46
00:02:51,960 --> 00:02:55,520
to use it as the starting
point for our second task.

47
00:02:56,080 --> 00:03:02,105
This time, let's ask Matrix to read those
notes and create a professional project report

48
00:03:02,140 --> 00:03:07,740
covering achievements, challenges,
progress, and next steps.

49
00:03:08,300 --> 00:03:13,240
Then, turn that report into an
interactive dashboard with a dark theme,

50
00:03:13,640 --> 00:03:15,305
charts, and status cards.

51
00:03:15,340 --> 00:03:19,240
We're asking it to use HTML, CSS,

52
00:03:20,200 --> 00:03:23,020
and JavaScript, with no external dependencies.

53
00:03:24,040 --> 00:03:27,200
Again, we send the request and start the task.

54
00:03:27,740 --> 00:03:31,780
While this one runs, notice how
we've broken the work into two tasks.

55
00:03:32,620 --> 00:03:34,040
First, create the notes.

56
00:03:35,040 --> 00:03:38,540
Then, use those notes to build
the report and dashboard.

57
00:03:39,340 --> 00:03:41,485
The second task has a clear starting point,

58
00:03:41,520 --> 00:03:45,160
and we have something
concrete to check at the end.

59
00:03:46,120 --> 00:03:49,120
That's a useful habit when
working with AI: give it context,

60
00:03:49,500 --> 00:03:52,200
describe the result you want,
and review what comes back.

61
00:03:52,700 --> 00:03:55,820
A finished task is a good
moment to take a closer look.

62
00:03:56,160 --> 00:03:58,580
Let's open the files and see what we've got.

63
00:03:58,960 --> 00:04:00,240
First, here's the report.

64
00:04:00,700 --> 00:04:03,485
We can read it as text or
open the formatted preview.

65
00:04:03,520 --> 00:04:08,525
It brings the project updates
together into a structured overview,

66
00:04:08,560 --> 00:04:12,540
with achievements, challenges, and next steps.

67
00:04:13,500 --> 00:04:16,080
And here's the dashboard,
running in the browser.

68
00:04:16,860 --> 00:04:20,485
We've got summary cards, charts,
and a timeline of updates.

69
00:04:20,520 --> 00:04:25,180
Using the navigation, we can
explore the project's achievements,

70
00:04:25,380 --> 00:04:27,880
challenges, progress, and next steps.

71
00:04:28,320 --> 00:04:32,300
So we started with a simple
request and created project notes.

72
00:04:33,100 --> 00:04:37,340
Then we used those notes to produce
a report and a working dashboard.

73
00:04:38,140 --> 00:04:42,700
Along the way, we could follow the
execution and inspect the finished files.

74
00:04:43,600 --> 00:04:47,985
That's Matrix: an AI Brain
working with an authorized Hand,

75
00:04:48,020 --> 00:04:51,220
with the process and the
results there for you to review.

76
00:04:51,880 --> 00:04:53,540
Separate the Brain from the Hand.

77
00:04:53,920 --> 00:04:55,020
Keep control of both.
